    <p><strong>Last updated:</strong> April 2026</p>

    <h2>Our commitment</h2>
    <p>Accessibility is a baseline on every site we design and build, including this one. Our working standard is <strong>WCAG 2.2 Level AA</strong>; the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines from the W3C. This covers things like color contrast, keyboard navigation, screen-reader support, focus visibility, and clear labeling. It's the same standard we write into our client agreements.</p>

    <h2>What we do on every site</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Real contrast ratios.</strong> We verify every text/background combination against WCAG AA (4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text). We don't just eyeball it.</li>
      <li><strong>Full keyboard navigation.</strong> Every interactive control is reachable by <kbd>Tab</kbd> and operable by keyboard alone. Focus rings are visible and predictable.</li>
      <li><strong>Semantic HTML.</strong> Headings in order, landmarks labeled, lists that are actually lists, buttons that are actually buttons. Screen readers get a clean structure to work with.</li>
      <li><strong>Skip links and landmarks.</strong> "Skip to content" appears for keyboard users. Each region of the page is labeled with an appropriate ARIA role or landmark.</li>
      <li><strong>Image alt text.</strong> Decorative SVGs are hidden from assistive technology with <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>. Meaningful images get real alt text.</li>
      <li><strong>Motion respected.</strong> Animations honor <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>. If your system says slow things down, we do.</li>
      <li><strong>Forms with proper labels.</strong> Every field has a visible label, not just placeholder text. Errors are announced and explained in plain language.</li>
      <li><strong>Light and dark themes.</strong> Color pairs meet contrast targets in both modes.</li>
      <li><strong>44px touch targets.</strong> Interactive elements are comfortably large on a phone.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we've tested</h2>
    <p>On this site specifically, we've tested with:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Keyboard-only navigation from top to bottom of every page</li>
      <li>VoiceOver (macOS &amp; iOS) and NVDA (Windows)</li>
      <li>Browser zoom up to 200% without horizontal scrolling</li>
      <li>Color-contrast analyzers on every text/background combo in both themes</li>
      <li>Automated audits via Lighthouse, axe DevTools, and WAVE</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Known limitations</h2>
    <p>We're not perfect and we don't pretend to be. If you find something that doesn't work, please tell us. We fix accessibility issues on the same day we hear about them whenever possible. usually within one business day.</p>

